===========================
📦 DATABASE INDEXING — MASTER NOTES (FAANG LEVEL)
===========================

🔥 First Principles (INTUITION)
--------------------------------
Soch ek library 📚 hai jisme millions of books hain.

👉 Without index:
   - Har baar tumhe book dhoondhne ke liye full scan karna padega
   - Time = O(N)

👉 With index:
   - Tumhare paas ek "shortcut map" hai
   - Time drastically reduce ho jata hai

👉 Real DB:
   - Table = actual data
   - Index = extra data structure for fast lookup

⚠️ Tradeoff:
   Faster Reads ✅
   Slower Writes ❌ (kyunki index bhi update karna padta hai)


=====================================================
1️⃣ HASH INDEX (In-Memory HashMap Style)
=====================================================

🧠 INTUITION:
-------------
Direct mapping: key → memory address

Jaise:
Phonebook = name → phone number

👉 Hash function -> instant location

-------------------------------------
⚙️ HOW IT WORKS:
-------------------------------------
key → hash(key) → bucket → value

-------------------------------------
⏱️ COMPLEXITY:
-------------------------------------
Read:  O(1)
Write: O(1)

-------------------------------------
❌ PROBLEM (VERY IMPORTANT):
-------------------------------------
👉 NO ORDERING
👉 Hence: RANGE QUERY IMPOSSIBLE

Example:
Find users with age between 20–30 ❌

-------------------------------------
📦 STORAGE:
-------------------------------------
👉 Mostly in-memory (RAM)
👉 Why?
   Hash access requires random access → disk is slow

-------------------------------------
⚠️ DURABILITY PROBLEM:
-------------------------------------
Memory crash → data gone 😱

-------------------------------------
💡 SOLUTION: WAL (Write Ahead Log)
-------------------------------------
👉 Every write first goes to disk log

Append-only log:
[INSERT A]
[INSERT B]

👉 Then update hashmap

👉 Crash recovery:
   Replay WAL

-------------------------------------
🌍 REAL WORLD USE:
-------------------------------------
👉 Redis (in-memory DB)
👉 Memcached
👉 Caching layers

-------------------------------------
📌 WHEN TO USE:
-------------------------------------
✔️ Ultra-fast reads needed
✔️ Exact key lookup
❌ NOT for range queries


=====================================================
2️⃣ B-TREE INDEX (DISK FRIENDLY KING 👑)
=====================================================

🧠 INTUITION:
-------------
Binary tree ka optimized version for DISK

👉 Problem:
Disk seek is expensive 😵

👉 Solution:
Make tree "wide" not "tall"

-------------------------------------
⚙️ STRUCTURE:
-------------------------------------
- Multi-way tree (not binary)
- Each node stores MANY keys
- Stored in disk pages (like 4KB blocks)

Example:
| 10 | 20 | 30 |
 /    |     \
...  ...   ...

-------------------------------------
🎯 WHY WIDE TREE?
-------------------------------------
👉 Less height → fewer disk reads

Height ≈ log_fanout(N)

-------------------------------------
⏱️ COMPLEXITY:
-------------------------------------
Read:  O(log N)
Write: O(log N)

BUT:
👉 Disk I/O dominates cost

-------------------------------------
📌 KEY FEATURE:
-------------------------------------
✔️ SORTED ORDER maintained

-------------------------------------
💥 RANGE QUERY MAGIC:
-------------------------------------
Find users age 20–30:

👉 Go to 20
👉 Traverse sequentially till 30

🔥 SUPER FAST

-------------------------------------
⚠️ WRITES ARE EXPENSIVE:
-------------------------------------
👉 Node split
👉 Rebalancing
👉 Disk rewrite

-------------------------------------
💡 DURABILITY:
-------------------------------------
Two approaches:

1) In-place update (simple, risky)
2) Copy-on-write (used in real DBs)

👉 Modify new page → update pointer

-------------------------------------
🌍 REAL WORLD USE:
-------------------------------------
👉 MySQL (InnoDB)
👉 PostgreSQL
👉 SQLite

-------------------------------------
📌 WHEN TO USE:
-------------------------------------
✔️ Range queries
✔️ Ordered data
✔️ General purpose DB


=====================================================
3️⃣ LSM TREE + SSTABLE (WRITE-OPTIMIZED BEAST 🚀)
=====================================================

🧠 INTUITION:
-------------
👉 Instead of updating disk again and again,
👉 Keep writing sequentially (append only)

"Sequential writes = fast on disk"

-------------------------------------
⚙️ COMPONENTS:
-------------------------------------

1) MemTable (in RAM)
   - Balanced tree (AVL / Red-Black)
   - Sorted

2) WAL (Write Ahead Log)
   - For durability

3) SSTables (on disk)
   - Sorted
   - Immutable (IMPORTANT)

-------------------------------------
✍️ WRITE FLOW:
-------------------------------------
1) Write to WAL (disk)
2) Insert into MemTable (RAM)

👉 When MemTable full:
   → Flush to disk as SSTable

-------------------------------------
📖 READ FLOW:
-------------------------------------
Search order:

1) MemTable
2) Latest SSTable
3) Older SSTables...

⚠️ WHY SLOW READ?
Because multiple files check karne padte hain

-------------------------------------
🗑️ DELETE (VERY IMPORTANT):
-------------------------------------
👉 No direct delete

Instead:
👉 Add "TOMBSTONE"

Example:
DELETE key=5
→ Insert: (5, deleted)

-------------------------------------
⚠️ PROBLEMS:
-------------------------------------
1) Many SSTables → slow reads
2) Duplicate keys
3) Disk space waste

-------------------------------------
💡 SOLUTIONS (OPTIMIZATIONS)
-------------------------------------

-------------------------------------
1) 🔍 SPARSE INDEX
-------------------------------------
👉 Not full index

Example:
Store every 100th key

👉 Binary search + scan

-------------------------------------
2) 🌸 BLOOM FILTER
-------------------------------------
👉 Fast probabilistic check

✔️ "Key NOT present" → 100% correct
❌ "Key present" → maybe

👉 Avoid unnecessary disk reads

-------------------------------------
3) 🔄 COMPACTION (VERY IMPORTANT)
-------------------------------------
👉 Merge SSTables

Before:
SST1: A=1, B=2
SST2: B=3

After:
Merged: A=1, B=3

✔️ Remove duplicates
✔️ Remove tombstones

⚠️ Tradeoff:
Extra CPU in background

-------------------------------------
⏱️ COMPLEXITY:
-------------------------------------
Write: VERY FAST (sequential)
Read:  Slower than B-Tree

-------------------------------------
🌍 REAL WORLD USE:
-------------------------------------
👉 Cassandra
👉 RocksDB
👉 LevelDB
👉 Bigtable

-------------------------------------
📌 WHEN TO USE:
-------------------------------------
✔️ Write-heavy systems
✔️ Logs, analytics
✔️ High throughput systems


=====================================================
⚔️ COMPARISON (INTERVIEW GOLD 🔥)
=====================================================

| Feature        | Hash Index | B-Tree     | LSM Tree       |
|---------------|-----------|-----------|----------------|
| Read Speed     | 🔥 Fast    | ⚡ Fast     | 🐢 Slower       |
| Write Speed    | 🔥 Fast    | 🐢 Slow     | 🚀 Very Fast    |
| Range Query    | ❌ No      | ✅ Yes      | ⚠️ Poor         |
| Storage        | RAM       | Disk       | RAM + Disk      |
| Durability     | WAL       | Built-in   | WAL             |
| Use Case       | Cache     | OLTP DB    | Write-heavy DB  |

=====================================================
🧠 INTERVIEW INSIGHTS (VERY IMPORTANT)
=====================================================

💬 If interviewer asks:
"Design a DB for heavy writes?"

👉 Answer:
Use LSM Tree

💬 If:
"Need range queries?"

👉 Answer:
B-Tree

💬 If:
"Need ultra fast lookup (cache)?"

👉 Answer:
Hash Index

-----------------------------------------------------

🎯 GOLDEN LINE (REMEMBER THIS):
--------------------------------
👉 B-Trees optimize READS
👉 LSM Trees optimize WRITES

=====================================================
🔥 FINAL ANALOGY (SUPER IMPORTANT)
=====================================================

Hash Index:
👉 Like dictionary (exact word search only)

B-Tree:
👉 Like sorted book index (range browsing possible)

LSM Tree:
👉 Like writing notes in diary and organizing later

=====================================================
🚀 WHAT TO LEARN NEXT:
=====================================================

1) B+ Trees (used in real DBs)
2) How MySQL uses clustered index
3) Read amplification vs write amplification
4) CAP theorem + consistency tradeoffs

===========================
END OF NOTES 🚀
